  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing an unweighted average of all outcomes across both lotteries (2400 Euros) and then counting how many outcomes in each lottery exceed this value. As computed, both lotteries have one outcome above 2400 (4000 in A, 6000 in B), so the reason would be indifferent. The individual, however, does not mention or imply any calculation of an average outcome. Instead, they focus on the number of positive outcomes in Lottery A and their combined probability (50%). They do not compare outcomes to a threshold value derived from an average. There is no evidence that they computed or used the mean of all outcomes. The justification is based on the possibility of winning in multiple ways (two outcomes vs one), not on magnitude relative to a computed average. Therefore, the information considered by the individual (number of winning outcomes, combined probability of winning) differs from the information required by the decision reason (unweighted mean of all outcomes, count of outcomes above that mean). The justification rule (favoring more outcomes above the average) is not reflected in the verbal reasoning. The individual’s focus is on the chance to win something, not on how outcomes compare to an average. Thus, there is no indication the decision reason was used.",
